\section{Tree placement}{

\code{position} only decides \strong{where the tree is drawn}, never where the labels
go.  The labels always stay on their usual side of the panel, so the tree can
sit on the opposite side without moving anything the reader is used to.
\itemize{
\item \code{"bottom"} / \code{"top"} (x axis): below or above the labels.  With facets the
tree is moved into the strip band, i.e. directly \strong{under the strip} and
above the panel, so strip, tree, labels and panel never overlap.  Use
\code{strip_side = "above"} to draw it outside the strip instead; either way a
small gap (\code{options(ggutils.tree_gap = 0.14)}, in centimetres) keeps the
branches from touching the strip.
\item \code{"left"} / \code{"right"} (y axis): left of the labels, or at the far end of the
panel row.  With facets, \code{"left"} draws one tree per \emph{visible} y axis;
\code{"right"} draws one at the end of every panel row.
}

A tree that has to sit away from the labels cannot travel through the axis
guide (a discrete secondary axis cannot carry a free panel's categories), so
it is drawn straight into the finished gtable, where the panel geometry is
known.  That is also what makes \code{facet_grid()} work: the row facet owns the y
axis and the column facet owns the x axis, and each tree is attached to the
strip of its own cell.
}

\section{Facets and \code{scales}}{

The \code{scales} argument of \code{facet_wrap()} / \code{facet_grid()} is read
automatically, so \code{free} never has to be written by hand:
\itemize{
\item \code{"fixed"}: one shared tree for every panel.
\item \code{"free_x"}: each panel column gets its own x tree.
\item \code{"free_y"}: y trees are recomputed per panel row.
\item \code{"free"}: both axes are recomputed panel by panel.
}

\code{facet_wrap()} draws one x tree per panel row (each under its own strip) and
one y tree per visible y axis; \code{facet_grid()} draws one x tree per panel and
one y tree per row.  Writing \code{free = FALSE} forces a single shared tree,
\code{free = TRUE} forces per-panel trees regardless of \code{scales}.
}

\section{Free facets}{

\code{facet_wrap(scales = "free")} gives every panel its own set of categories,
and with \code{free = TRUE} its own tree.  A discrete scale's limits are global,
so the categories are re-keyed per panel internally: each panel's focal
column becomes \code{"<panel>__ggcl__<category>"} and is ordered by that panel's
own \code{hclust()} leaf order.  The axis therefore really is in leaf order, a
standard tree can be drawn without a crossing branch, and the prefix never
reaches the printed labels.  Users never see the keys.

With an explicitly supplied matrix (\code{data = mat}) the axis stays in data
order and the panel tree falls back to merging only \strong{adjacent} clusters,
which keeps the layout planar at the cost of a shape that differs from a
plain \code{hclust()}.
}

\section{Deriving the matrix from the plot}{

\code{scale_x_cluster()} and \code{scale_y_cluster()} do not need a matrix.  When
\code{data} is omitted, the matrix is built from the plot data at draw time:
\enumerate{
\item the focal axis (\code{x} for \code{\link[=scale_x_cluster]{scale_x_cluster()}}, \code{y} for \code{\link[=scale_y_cluster]{scale_y_cluster()}})
provides the rows, the other positional axis provides the columns;
\item the values come from the first numeric aesthetic mapped among \code{fill},
\code{colour}, \code{size}, \code{linewidth}, \code{alpha} and \code{weight}; use the \code{value}
argument to pick another column;
\item with no numeric aesthetic, a continuous other axis is summarised per
category (\code{mean}, \code{sd}, \code{median}, \code{n}), and two categorical axes are
clustered on how often the categories co-occur (a message says so).
}

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{ggplot(df, aes(gene, sample, fill = value)) +
  geom_tile() +
  scale_x_cluster()                       # no matrix needed
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Missing focal/other combinations are filled with the row mean instead of
dropping the category, so an incomplete grid still keeps every label on the
axis.  \code{hclust} remains available for a pre-computed tree.

A guide on its own cannot change the order in which a scale maps categories
to positions: that order is fixed before the guide is built.  Therefore, to
both draw the tree \emph{and} re-order the axis, use \code{scale_x_cluster()} /
\code{scale_y_cluster()}.  If you attach \code{guide_axis_cluster()} directly with
\code{guides(x = ...)}, the tree is aligned to the categories but the data order
is left unchanged.
}

\section{Radial coordinates (\code{coord_radial()})}{

On a circular plot the theta (x) and r (y) axes are handled differently.

\strong{theta axis (x) -- ring dendrogram.}  The category labels keep their stock
\code{guide_axis_theta()} look and the tree becomes a \emph{ring dendrogram}: leaves
on a circle, every merge an arc at another radius, joined to its children by
radial stubs.  \code{type = "triangle"} draws straight chords to each merge
instead of arcs.
\itemize{
\item \code{position = "bottom"} (default) draws the tree \strong{inside the hole}: the
leaves sit on the inner circle and every merge steps towards the centre.
This needs \code{inner.radius > 0}; a pie without a hole has no room, so the
tree is skipped.
\item \code{position = "top"} draws it \strong{outside the circle}: leaves on the outer
circle, merges growing outwards.  With the default \code{ring_gap = NULL} the
leaf ring starts after the longest theta label, and extra padding is
inserted around every panel, so facet strips and neighbouring panels are
never touched.  Leave some \code{plot.margin} / device room for the tree.
}

\strong{r axis (y) -- partial arcs only.}  On a full circle the r axis is a plain
column, so \code{scale_y_cluster()} draws \strong{no} tree there.  With a partial arc
(\code{start}/\code{end}) ggplot2 puts the r labels on a radial ray and the tree
becomes an \strong{arc-shaped wedge dendrogram} at the other end of the arc, the
side without labels: the leaf position becomes a radius and the merge
height an angle, so every straight branch becomes a concentric arc and the
tree hugs the heat map.  \code{height} sets the angular width
(\code{min(pi/3, 0.30 * height)} radians).

\strong{Facets.}  Every panel gets its own tree, and with
\code{facet_wrap(scales = "free_x")} that tree is clustered from the panel's own
genes, so the panels differ.  With fixed scales one shared tree is used.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# per-sample z-scores: standardise every sample across the genes once
z <- local(\{
  m <- tapply(airway_heatmap$df$z,
              list(airway_heatmap$df$gene, airway_heatmap$df$sample), mean)
  m <- scale(m)                                # column-wise = per sample
  out <- as.data.frame(as.table(m))
  names(out) <- c("gene", "sample", "z_sample")
  merge(out, airway_heatmap$genes[c("gene", "gene_set")], by = "gene")
\})
d <- droplevels(z[z$gene \%in\% unique(z$gene)[1:16], ])
fill_scale <- scale_fill_gradient2(low = "#2166ac", mid = "white",
                                   high = "#b2182b", midpoint = 0,
                                   name = "z (sample)")

# (1) x tree inside the hole: bottom + inner.radius
ggplot(d, aes(gene, sample, fill = z_sample)) + geom_tile() +
  coord_radial(theta = "x", inner.radius = 0.55) +
  scale_x_cluster(method = "ward.D2", height = 1.2) +
  fill_scale

# (2) x tree outside the circle: position = "top"
ggplot(d, aes(gene, sample, fill = z_sample)) + geom_tile() +
  coord_radial(theta = "x") +
  scale_x_cluster(method = "ward.D2", height = 1, position = "top") +
  fill_scale

# (3) partial arc + r-axis tree at the label-free end of the arc
ggplot(d, aes(gene, sample, fill = z_sample)) + geom_tile() +
  coord_radial(theta = "x", start = -0.5 * pi, end = 0.5 * pi,
               inner.radius = 0.55) +
  scale_y_cluster(method = "ward.D2", height = 1.6) +
  fill_scale

# (4) facetted: one tree per panel, clustered per panel
ggplot(d, aes(gene, sample, fill = z_sample)) + geom_tile() +
  facet_wrap(~ gene_set, scales = "free_x") +
  coord_radial(theta = "x", inner.radius = 0.45) +
  scale_x_cluster(method = "ward.D2", height = 0.8) +
  fill_scale +
  theme_grey(base_size = 7)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Faceted free scales}{

With \code{facet_wrap(scales = "free_x")} (or \code{"free_y"}) every panel has its own
categories, so one pre-computed tree cannot fit all panels.  In that case
pass the clustering data to the guide itself and set \code{free = TRUE}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{ggplot(d, aes(gene, value)) +
  geom_point() +
  facet_wrap(~ cluster, scales = "free_x") +
  guides(x = guide_axis_cluster(data = mat, free = TRUE, method = "average"))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The guide then takes, in each panel, the rows of \code{mat} whose names match
that panel's categories, clusters that subset, and draws a tree sized for
exactly those leaves.  When the matrix was derived from the plot data (no
\code{data} argument), the per-panel matrix is rebuilt from that long data, so
combinations missing from a panel cannot become \code{NA} and drop a category.
The scale must leave the category order to the data (do \strong{not} set global
\code{limits}); if you also want the categories re-ordered per panel, use
\code{scale_x_cluster()}/\code{scale_y_cluster()} with \code{free = TRUE}, which re-orders
each panel's axis into that panel's leaf order for you.
}

\section{Canvas height}{

The tree row is a \strong{fixed} \code{height} (in cm), while panel rows are \verb{1null}.
When the fixed rows (trees + rotated axis labels + strips) need more room than
the device provides, gtable gives the null panel rows zero height and the
figure looks like trees and axis text only.  Leave roughly 2-3 cm of height
per panel row -- with two rows of faceted panels plus rotated labels, a
\code{fig.height} of 4 inches is already too short.  Either enlarge the device or
lower \code{height}.
}

\examples{
\dontrun{
library(ggplot2)

set.seed(1)
mat <- matrix(rnorm(12 * 5), nrow = 12,
              dimnames = list(paste0("g", 1:12), paste0("s", 1:5)))
long <- as.data.frame(as.table(mat))
names(long) <- c("gene", "sample", "value")

# x axis: genes are clustered and re-ordered, tree above the labels
ggplot(long, aes(gene, sample, fill = value)) +
  geom_tile() +
  scale_x_cluster(mat, method = "average", height = 1.2, col = "#b2182b") +
  scale_y_discrete(guide = guide_axis(show.tick.label = TRUE))

# y axis: same matrix, tree to the right of the labels
ggplot(long, aes(sample, gene, fill = value)) +
  geom_tile() +
  scale_y_cluster(mat, method = "ward.D2", height = 1.2, col = "#2166ac")

# No matrix at all: the tree is clustered from the plot data itself
ggplot(long, aes(gene, sample, fill = value)) +
  geom_tile() +
  scale_x_cluster(method = "average", position = "top")

# Tree away from the labels: x labels stay at the bottom, the tree moves
# under the strip; y labels stay on the left, the tree moves to the right
ggplot(long, aes(gene, sample, fill = value)) +
  geom_point() +
  facet_wrap(~ sample, scales = "free_x") +
  scale_x_cluster(position = "top", height = 1)

ggplot(long, aes(gene, sample, fill = value)) +
  geom_point() +
  facet_wrap(~ sample, scales = "free_y") +
  scale_y_cluster(position = "left", height = 1)

# Both axes at once; `scales` is picked up from the facet
ggplot(long, aes(gene, sample, fill = value)) +
  geom_point() +
  facet_wrap(~ sample, scales = "free") +
  scale_x_cluster(position = "top", height = 1) +
  scale_y_cluster(position = "left", height = 1)

# facet_grid: the row facet owns y, the column facet owns x.
# Group both axes so the grid stays small; drop = FALSE keeps the empty
# levels visible, which is what makes "fixed" and "free_y" differ.
grid_df <- long
grid_df$g_half <- factor(ifelse(grid_df$gene \%in\% paste0("g", 1:6),
                                "g1-g6", "g7-g12"))
grid_df$s_half <- factor(ifelse(grid_df$sample \%in\% c("s1", "s2"),
                                "s1-s2", "s3-s5"))
ggplot(grid_df, aes(gene, sample, fill = value)) +
  geom_point() +
  facet_grid(s_half ~ g_half, scales = "free", drop = FALSE) +
  scale_x_cluster(position = "top", height = 1) +
  scale_y_cluster(position = "left", height = 1)
}

}
